  "Question": "The sequence \\(a_1,\\) \\(a_2,\\) \\(a_3,\\) \\(\\ldots\\) satisfies \\(a_1 = 19,\\) \\(a_9 = 99,\\) and, for all \\(n \\ge 3,\\) \\(a_n\\) is the arithmetic mean of the first \\(n - 1\\) terms. Find \\(a_2.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(29\\)",
    "B": "\\(59\\)",
    "C": "\\(79\\)",
    "D": "\\(99\\)",
    "E": "\\(179\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The useful observation is that adding a list's own mean does not change its mean. Indeed, if \\(k\\) numbers have mean \\(m\\), their sum is \\(km\\). Appending \\(m\\) gives the new mean\n\\[\n\\frac{km+m}{k+1}=m.\n\\]\nLet \\(m=(19+a_2)/2\\), the mean of the first two terms. The rule gives \\(a_3=m\\). Appending this term preserves the mean, so \\(a_4=m\\); repeating the same reasoning gives\n\\[\na_3=a_4=\\cdots=a_9=m.\n\\]\nSince \\(a_9=99\\), we have\n\\[\n\\frac{19+a_2}{2}=99,\n\\qquad a_2=198-19=179.\n\\]\nWith these values, the first two terms have mean 99, and every subsequent appended term is 99, so the recurrence is satisfied. Therefore \\(a_2=\\boxed{179}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
